---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { VALUE_SENTENCE } from '../../content/home';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const P = K.P;
const name = SITE_NAMES.en;
const title = 'Medicare Levy Surcharge Calculator 2026: Who Publishes It';
const description = `${name} is published by Radif Partners: the 2026-27 surcharge, rebate and LHC loading from ATO rules, set against public policy prices in AUD.`;
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About {name}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Each year around 1 July, and again at tax time, many Australians ask the same question: my income has crossed the line, do I pay the Medicare levy surcharge or do I buy hospital cover? The ATO explains the thresholds and the rates very well, and rightly says nothing about prices. The pages that do talk about prices belong to insurers and comparison services, and they end on a quote form, a phone call or a short list of partner funds. Some still print the {P.mls.prior.year} thresholds in the {P.incomeYear} year.</p>
    <p><strong>{VALUE_SENTENCE}</strong></p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public rules. It is not a government agency and is not affiliated with the Australian Taxation Office, Services Australia, the Department of Health, the Private Health Insurance Ombudsman or any health insurer. It is not a financial adviser or an insurance broker, sells no policy, takes no commission or referral fee, and has no partner to send you to.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we rely on</h2>
    <p>For the surcharge and the rebate: the ATO pages on income thresholds and rates for {P.incomeYear} (<Fragment set:html={src('ato_mls_rates', 'ato.gov.au')} />), the M2 question of the tax return instructions, and the Department of Health circular that sets the rebate adjustment factor each 1 April. For the loading: the Lifetime Health Cover rules and worked examples on <Fragment set:html={src('phio_lhc', 'PrivateHealth.gov.au')} />. For prices: the product file the Ombudsman publishes every month on <Fragment set:html={src('datagov_phi', 'data.gov.au')} />, used under the Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Australia licence.</p>
    <h2>What the site leaves out</h2>
    <p>We do not judge whether a policy suits your health needs, list waiting periods, gap payments or the hospitals an insurer has agreements with, or compare extras cover. Our premiums are the published base premiums, without the age-based discount some insurers offer to people under 30 or any fund-specific promotion. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every filter and formula; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how corrections are made.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for corrections and sources. We answer questions about a rule or a figure, not about your personal case: the ATO decides your surcharge and your insurer applies your rebate and your loading.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
